9 #uuid "d64a47db-1f98-5942-95f8-0a5fe8a1d806" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426168 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426168 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163203" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426168 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426168 #edb/ref 1071 "A rectangular plate of mass $M\\,\\text{kg}$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 4,\\,\\,1\\le y\\le 3\\},$ where $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $x$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426168 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a005e400-255d-52bf-b2df-866a68951503" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426168 #edb/ref 1074 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=3\\cdot 2=6.$ \n\nSince the plate has uniform density, every unit of area contains the same amount of mass. Therefore, the mass density is constant and is given by the total mass divided by the total area:\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{6}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{y}^{2}[x]_{1}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{1}^{3}3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{[{y}^{3}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}(27-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{13M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia is $\\frac{13M}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426169 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426159 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426169 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163202" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426169 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426169 #edb/ref 1071 "A rectangular plate of mass $M\\,\\text{kg}$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 3\\},$ where $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $y$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426169 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a576d72d-3059-5635-bf8f-38bb7570610e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426169 #edb/ref 1074 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=1\\cdot 3=3.$ \n\nSince the plate has uniform density, every unit of area contains the same amount of mass. Therefore, the mass density is constant and is given by the total mass divided by the total area:\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{3}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{7}{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{7M}{9}[y]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia is $\\frac{7M}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426172 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426172 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163145" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426172 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426172 #edb/ref 1071 "A rectangular plate occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,2\\le x\\le 4,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}$\nwith mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},$ where $x$ and $y$ area measured in meters. Find the moment of inertia of this lamina about the $x$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426172 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d5a11202-6be7-5636-bb7a-f06e82bc2539" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426172 #edb/ref 1074 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2}^{4}{y}^{2}\\cdot x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{2}^{4}x{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{4}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{2}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{6}{5}{y}^{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}-0 \\\\  & =1.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia of the lamina about the $x$-axis is $1.2\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428555 #edb/ref 1030 0.3333333333333333 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592